OURS (not proposed to Guillaume Duboc). Three functions for the reconstruction of
interfaces (Section 9 of the paper; lib/process_types/reconstruct*), none of which
changes tallying:
- tally_normalize/3 (Tally.normalize_constraint/3) exposes the first step of tallying on
  one constraint: the alternatives of its normal form, each a list of bounds
  {lower, variable, upper}. The reconstruction reads in them the bounds that a
  constraint entails (the decompositions of Appendix D of the paper).
- substitute_top/2 substitutes variables at the top level of a type only, not below its
  type constructors: used to collapse the chains of bounds between unknowns.
- solve_equations/2 (Tally.solve_guarded/2) solves a guarded system of equations
  variable = type, with the recursive nodes named by the caller: it builds the system
  as solve_system/2 (patch 09) does for the solutions of tallying.
